Answer: ```java
public class PrimitiveDataTypes {
public static void main(String[] args) {
byte a = 10;
short b = 128;
int c = 32768;
long d = 2147483648L;
float e = 3.147F;
double g = 22.3456789012;
char h = 'A';
System.out.println(a + " " + b + " " + c + " " +
d + " " + e + " " + g + " " + h);
} }
```

Line-by-Line Explanation

```java
byte a = 10;
```

Declares a byte variable and stores the value 10.

```java
short b = 128;
```

Declares a short variable because 128 is outside the valid range of byte.

```java
int c = 32768;
```

Declares an int variable to store a larger integer.

```java
long d = 2147483648L;
```

Declares a long variable. The suffix L tells Java that the literal is of type long.

```java
float e = 3.147F;
```

Declares a float variable. The suffix F is required because decimal literals are treated as double by default.

```java
double g = 22.3456789012;
```

Declares a double variable for higher precision decimal values.

```java
char h = 'A';
```

Declares a char variable and stores a single character enclosed in single quotes.

```java
System.out.println(...);
```

Prints the values of all primitive variables. This program demonstrates how different primitive data types are declared and used in Java.

Answer: Java requires suffixes for certain numeric literals: L (or l) is used with long literals. F (or f) is used with float literals because decimal literals are considered double by default. Examples:

```java
long num = 2147483648L;
float price = 3.147F;
```

Without these suffixes, Java treats the values as different data types and compilation may fail.
